     "text": "Morphology — see comparison tables."
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      41.76,
      631.44,
      275.76,
      639.84
     ],
     "text": "Australian subterranean clover varieties are distinguished"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      41.76,
      641.28,
      258.96,
      649.68
     ],
     "text": "from one another by a combination of relatively stable"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      41.52,
      650.88,
      270.72,
      659.28
     ],
     "text": "characteristics (Collins ef a/, 1984). The main descriptors"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      41.76,
      660.96,
      234.24,
      669.12
     ],
     "text": "are flower and stipule colour, leaf markings and"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      42.0,
      670.8,
      254.4,
      678.72
     ],
     "text": "pubescence on leaflets and stems. These characters,"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      42.0,
      680.64,
      271.2,
      689.04
     ],
     "text": "however, are not entirely uniform throughout any variety"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      42.0,
      690.48,
      277.44,
      698.64
     ],
     "text": "and may also vary in response to environment (particularly"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      41.76,
      700.56,
      171.36,
      708.72
     ],
     "text": "pigmentation with temperature)."
    }
   ],
   "page": 1,
   "source": "tesseract-psm3",
   "text": "Morphology — see comparison tables.\nAustralian subterranean clover varieties are distinguished\nfrom one another by a combination of relatively stable\ncharacteristics (Collins ef a/, 1984). The main descriptors\nare flower and stipule colour, leaf markings and\npubescence on leaflets and stems. These characters,\nhowever, are not entirely uniform throughout any variety\nand may also vary in response to environment (particularly\npigmentation with temperature).",
